{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfPjnLRQMF4h1ckfA8Qbe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Blocked on one thing, and it's the important one: **WebSearch isn't approved in this session**, so I have no way to retrieve a single source. Please approve web access and I'll build the sheet properly.\n\nI'm not going to write you citations from memory. On this specific topic that would be malpractice rather than caution — the 1811 comet vintage is one of the most folklore-saturated subjects in wine writing, and the plausible-sounding citation is the exact failure mode. A fabricated author/title/year under your byline is the kind of thing that outlives the piece.\n\n**What I can tell you now, unverified, to aim the research** (treat every line as a research target, not a fact):\n\n- **The astronomy is the solid ground.** The Great Comet of 1811 (C/1811 F1), Flaugergues' discovery, and its unusually long naked-eye visibility are documented in astronomical literature with real, citable sources. This part will verify cleanly.\n- **The 1811 growing season** — hot, dry, exceptional ripeness across multiple European regions — should be traceable to 19th-century viticultural records, but the commonly repeated version is usually secondhand.\n- **Yquem 1811 is where confusion starts.** The eye-catching figures attached to it are *modern auction* prices, not period prices. Pieces routinely blur the two. Decide which you're claiming.\n- **The Veuve Clicquot comet-on-the-cork story** is the highest-risk item in your draft, whatever your draft says. It's charming, universally repeated, and I have not seen it tied to a primary source. Needs a contemporary document or it gets attributed (\"the house's telling\") rather than asserted.\n- **Cognac is your strongest angle and your biggest trap.** \"1811\" became a near-decorative marketing claim on brandy for a century-plus after the fact. Producers \"trading on it\" is arguably more a story about label fraud than about wine.\n\n**On period price claims specifically** — this is the hardest category on the page. Most figures circulating online trace back to modern trade blogs citing nothing. Real period prices come from auction catalogues, merchant ledgers, regional classifications, and contemporary newspaper advertising. If those aren't reachable, the honest move is to publish fewer price claims, well-sourced, rather than a full set of soft ones.\n\nApprove WebSearch and I'll work each claim independently, then hand you the sheet in three marked tiers: verified with printable citations, unverifiable-and-flagged, and cut-with-reason. I'll tell you plainly which is which — including if the answer is that a beloved detail doesn't survive checking.\n\nIf web access can't be opened tonight, say so and I'll instead give you a structured research checklist with the specific sources to pull, so you can verify by hand before your deadline."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":882,"cache_read_input_tokens":33953,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":882},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d6f6485d-14da-4898-ae16-541494dcfc30","uuid":"e6d62cd8-13f1-4c97-b2e9-9da1e3284de6","timestamp":"2026-09-25T07:10:04.133Z","request_id":"req_011CfPjnKrBXyqH7VWQTyMYN"}
